1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,986
Two o'clock in the afternoon,

2
00:00:02,006 --> 00:00:04,610
the fourteenth of December 1944.

3
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:08,394
Puerto Princesa, on the Philippine island

4
00:00:08,414 --> 00:00:10,330
of Palawan.

5
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:13,150
An air raid alarm sounds over a prison

6
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:15,266
camp holding one hundred and fifty

7
00:00:15,286 --> 00:00:16,676
American prisoners of war.

8
00:00:16,696 --> 00:00:19,144
The guards shout that hundreds of American

9
00:00:19,164 --> 00:00:20,202
planes are coming.

10
00:00:20,222 --> 00:00:22,317
And for the first time ever,

11
00:00:22,337 --> 00:00:24,785
the guards force every prisoner down into

12
00:00:24,805 --> 00:00:25,605
the shelters.

13
00:00:26,925 --> 00:00:30,425
The men in the trenches let themselves hope.

14
00:00:30,445 --> 00:00:32,851
American planes over Palawan meant

15
00:00:32,871 --> 00:00:35,194
American ships close behind.

16
00:00:35,214 --> 00:00:37,900
Sergeant Douglas Bogue, a Marine,

17
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:40,838
later swore that they thought it might be

18
00:00:40,858 --> 00:00:41,940
the real thing.

19
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,511
Then, through the narrow openings of their

20
00:00:44,531 --> 00:00:46,348
shelters, they watched about sixty

21
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:48,185
Japanese soldiers walk toward them.

22
00:00:48,205 --> 00:00:50,388
They carried machine guns and rifles.

23
00:00:50,408 --> 00:00:52,245
Some of them carried buckets.

24
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:56,245
Out of one hundred and fifty men,

25
00:00:56,265 --> 00:00:56,985
eleven lived.

26
00:00:57,005 --> 00:00:59,575
This is the documented story of the

27
00:00:59,595 --> 00:01:00,234
Palawan massacre:

28
00:01:00,254 --> 00:01:02,630
how eleven men got out, through a fence,

29
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:05,326
down a cliff and across five miles of open

30
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:08,021
water, and how what they told the Army may

31
00:01:08,041 --> 00:01:09,818
have helped save five hundred other

32
00:01:09,838 --> 00:01:11,335
prisoners a few weeks later.

33
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,920
The story starts in August 1942.

34
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:19,128
Four months after the surrender of Bataan

35
00:01:19,148 --> 00:01:21,021
and Corregidor, about three hundred and

36
00:01:21,041 --> 00:01:22,914
fifty American prisoners were shipped from

37
00:01:22,934 --> 00:01:26,100
the camp at Cabanatuan to Palawan,

38
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:28,764
a long, narrow island at the western edge

39
00:01:28,784 --> 00:01:29,763
of the Philippines.

40
00:01:29,783 --> 00:01:32,761
Their job was to build an airfield for the

41
00:01:32,781 --> 00:01:33,780
Japanese, by hand.

42
00:01:35,075 --> 00:01:38,193
For two years they cut down jungle with

43
00:01:38,213 --> 00:01:40,546
axes, crushed coral and poured concrete

44
00:01:40,566 --> 00:01:42,115
under the tropical sun.

45
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:46,036
They lived on wormy rice and soup made of vines.

46
00:01:46,056 --> 00:01:49,174
A camp cook broke two men's arms for

47
00:01:49,194 --> 00:01:49,958
picking papayas.

48
00:01:49,978 --> 00:01:53,017
One Marine, Glenn McDole, had his appendix

49
00:01:53,037 --> 00:01:54,565
taken out without anaesthetic.

50
00:01:54,585 --> 00:01:58,050
And the camp had one rule above all others.

51
00:01:58,070 --> 00:01:59,986
For every prisoner who escaped,

52
00:02:00,006 --> 00:02:01,555
ten would be executed.

53
00:02:02,875 --> 00:02:05,528
In September 1944, one hundred and

54
00:02:05,548 --> 00:02:09,008
fifty-nine of the men were shipped back to Manila.

55
00:02:09,028 --> 00:02:13,355
One hundred and fifty stayed behind on Palawan.

56
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:15,598
Nobody knew it yet, but the ones who left

57
00:02:15,618 --> 00:02:16,615
were the lucky ones.

58
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:21,090
On the nineteenth of October 1944,

59
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:23,510
American B-24 bombers appeared over

60
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:25,388
Palawan for the first time.

61
00:02:25,408 --> 00:02:28,183
One pilot flew low over the camp and

62
00:02:28,203 --> 00:02:30,280
waggled his wings at the prisoners,

63
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:33,775
then went on to bomb the airfield they had built.

64
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:36,221
On the twenty-eighth the bombers came back.

65
00:02:36,241 --> 00:02:39,017
The guards took it out on the prisoners.

66
00:02:39,037 --> 00:02:41,463
After every raid, an Army report said

67
00:02:41,483 --> 00:02:43,230
later, the treatment got worse.

68
00:02:44,575 --> 00:02:46,928
After the raids, the prisoners were

69
00:02:46,948 --> 00:02:49,300
ordered to dig air raid shelters.

70
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:52,068
Three trenches, five feet deep and four

71
00:02:52,088 --> 00:02:55,232
feet wide, each long enough for fifty men,

72
00:02:55,252 --> 00:02:57,604
covered over with logs and earth.

73
00:02:57,624 --> 00:03:00,768
The Japanese wanted one entrance for each trench.

74
00:03:00,788 --> 00:03:03,140
The American officers argued until they

75
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,326
were allowed two.

76
00:03:04,346 --> 00:03:06,699
Marine Rufus Smith noticed something else.

77
00:03:06,719 --> 00:03:10,258
Just over the fence, a little way from the

78
00:03:10,278 --> 00:03:12,255
trenches, stood drums of gasoline.

79
00:03:13,675 --> 00:03:16,369
The guards had been saying it for months.

80
00:03:16,389 --> 00:03:19,083
Smith later swore that one of them told

81
00:03:19,103 --> 00:03:21,118
the prisoners that if the Americans

82
00:03:21,138 --> 00:03:23,832
landed, they would never live to see the

83
00:03:23,852 --> 00:03:24,511
American Army.

84
00:03:24,531 --> 00:03:26,886
On the tenth of December, a Japanese

85
00:03:26,906 --> 00:03:29,600
lookout spotted a huge American convoy at sea.

86
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:31,635
It was heading for another island,

87
00:03:31,655 --> 00:03:33,395
Mindoro.

88
00:03:33,415 --> 00:03:36,606
But on Palawan, the Japanese expected a landing.

89
00:03:36,626 --> 00:03:38,875
According to the historian Stephen Moore,

90
00:03:38,895 --> 00:03:41,522
on the thirteenth of December an order

91
00:03:41,542 --> 00:03:44,168
came down from the Japanese air division

92
00:03:44,188 --> 00:03:44,547
commander.

93
00:03:44,567 --> 00:03:46,815
If the prisoners were harboring hostile

94
00:03:46,835 --> 00:03:49,841
feelings, they were to be disposed of at

95
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:50,995
the appropriate time.

96
00:03:52,650 --> 00:03:55,415
At about two in the morning on the

97
00:03:55,435 --> 00:03:57,155
fourteenth, the prisoners heard Japanese

98
00:03:57,175 --> 00:03:59,243
soldiers moving around outside the compound.

99
00:03:59,263 --> 00:04:02,376
At dawn they went out to work as usual,

100
00:04:02,396 --> 00:04:05,161
but the soldiers were in full field gear.

101
00:04:05,181 --> 00:04:07,945
At noon, all the work parties were called

102
00:04:07,965 --> 00:04:09,337
back to the camp.

103
00:04:09,357 --> 00:04:12,122
The guards called one air raid alarm after

104
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:12,470
another.

105
00:04:12,490 --> 00:04:16,014
Bogue saw two American fighters high overhead.

106
00:04:16,034 --> 00:04:17,984
Fern Barta, a Navy radioman,

107
00:04:18,004 --> 00:04:21,550
later swore that he never saw a single plane.

108
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,280
At about two o'clock, the men were forced

109
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:25,880
under cover.

110
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:30,004
Bogue wrote that it was the first time the

111
00:04:30,024 --> 00:04:32,544
Japanese had ever made them do that.

112
00:04:32,564 --> 00:04:33,996
Then the soldiers came.

113
00:04:34,016 --> 00:04:36,900
They went to the shelter of A Company

114
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,441
first, where about forty men were crowded

115
00:04:39,461 --> 00:04:39,804
together.

116
00:04:39,824 --> 00:04:42,707
They threw a lit torch into the entrance,

117
00:04:42,727 --> 00:04:45,611
followed by a bucket or two of gasoline.

118
00:04:45,631 --> 00:04:46,720
The gasoline exploded.

119
00:04:48,125 --> 00:04:51,059
Men ran out of the shelter on fire,

120
00:04:51,079 --> 00:04:52,906
straight into machine gun fire.

121
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:55,122
Four American officers who shared a

122
00:04:55,142 --> 00:04:57,707
foxhole near A Company, among them the

123
00:04:57,727 --> 00:04:59,923
camp's doctor, never had a chance.

124
00:04:59,943 --> 00:05:02,508
In the other shelters, the prisoners heard

125
00:05:02,528 --> 00:05:03,985
the explosion and understood.

126
00:05:04,005 --> 00:05:07,292
Bogue turned to the two men in his

127
00:05:07,312 --> 00:05:09,279
foxhole, which was close to the fence.

128
00:05:09,299 --> 00:05:10,982
Our only chance, he told them,

129
00:05:11,002 --> 00:05:12,705
is to get through the fence.

130
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:16,231
Some men clawed through the barbed wire

131
00:05:16,251 --> 00:05:17,489
with their bare hands.

132
00:05:17,509 --> 00:05:20,005
Others dug out through the far end of

133
00:05:20,025 --> 00:05:20,634
their trench.

134
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:22,836
Barta crawled through a drain that ran

135
00:05:22,856 --> 00:05:25,981
under the fence and came out at the top of

136
00:05:26,001 --> 00:05:28,811
a cliff, forty or fifty feet above the beach.

137
00:05:28,831 --> 00:05:31,642
Thirty or forty men made it down to the

138
00:05:31,662 --> 00:05:32,900
rocks along the shore.

139
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:35,750
For most of them, it was not far enough.

140
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:40,938
The soldiers came down to the beach after them.

141
00:05:40,958 --> 00:05:43,666
They hunted through the rocks with rifles,

142
00:05:43,686 --> 00:05:44,835
bayonets and grenades.

143
00:05:44,855 --> 00:05:47,953
They blasted men out of crevices with dynamite.

144
00:05:47,973 --> 00:05:50,682
A barge moved slowly along the shore,

145
00:05:50,702 --> 00:05:53,410
firing at anyone who tried to swim.

146
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:56,898
Bogue wedged himself into the rocks and

147
00:05:56,918 --> 00:05:57,621
lay still.

148
00:05:57,641 --> 00:05:59,789
He stayed there for seven hours,

149
00:05:59,809 --> 00:06:00,609
listening.

150
00:06:01,525 --> 00:06:03,276
Army private Eugene Nielsen covered

151
00:06:03,296 --> 00:06:04,693
himself with coconut husks.

152
00:06:04,713 --> 00:06:07,527
The soldiers found him, and he played dead

153
00:06:07,547 --> 00:06:08,944
until they moved on.

154
00:06:08,964 --> 00:06:11,424
He crawled into a cove where fifteen

155
00:06:11,444 --> 00:06:12,487
Americans were hiding.

156
00:06:12,507 --> 00:06:15,321
Eight of them were shot from the barge.

157
00:06:15,341 --> 00:06:17,801
When the soldiers came for the rest,

158
00:06:17,821 --> 00:06:19,572
Nielsen ran into the sea.

159
00:06:19,592 --> 00:06:21,698
Bullets hit him in the leg,

160
00:06:21,718 --> 00:06:24,177
in the head and in the ribs.

161
00:06:24,197 --> 00:06:25,240
He kept swimming.

162
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:27,366
A strong current carried him out,

163
00:06:27,386 --> 00:06:29,491
and at three in the afternoon,

164
00:06:29,511 --> 00:06:32,325
the Army report says, he reached the far

165
00:06:32,345 --> 00:06:34,825
side of a bay five miles wide.

166
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,320
On the cliff above, Army sergeant Willie

167
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:39,915
Balchus and another prisoner,

168
00:06:39,935 --> 00:06:42,610
Elmo Deal, were cornered by three soldiers

169
00:06:42,630 --> 00:06:43,380
carrying torches.

170
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:47,601
Balchus hit the first one on the head with

171
00:06:47,621 --> 00:06:50,283
a big piece of coral, and the two

172
00:06:50,303 --> 00:06:51,623
Americans jumped the others.

173
00:06:51,643 --> 00:06:52,964
More soldiers came running.

174
00:06:52,984 --> 00:06:54,640
Deal went over the cliff.

175
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:58,220
Balchus ran down the beach and into the water.

176
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:02,113
If you want more prisoner of war stories

177
00:07:02,133 --> 00:07:04,830
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

178
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:05,299
Frontlines.

179
00:07:05,319 --> 00:07:08,130
It helps us keep finding them.

180
00:07:09,750 --> 00:07:12,100
At about nine that night, Bogue crept out

181
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,305
of the rocks and found three more

182
00:07:14,325 --> 00:07:14,620
survivors:

183
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:18,290
Barta, Edwin Petry and Alberto Pacheco.

184
00:07:18,310 --> 00:07:21,373
A fifth man, a Marine named Donald Martyn,

185
00:07:21,393 --> 00:07:22,143
joined them.

186
00:07:22,163 --> 00:07:24,455
There was only one way out.

187
00:07:24,475 --> 00:07:27,538
The five men waded into the black water

188
00:07:27,558 --> 00:07:29,079
and started to swim.

189
00:07:29,099 --> 00:07:30,717
Behind them, Bogue later swore,

190
00:07:30,737 --> 00:07:32,510
they could see fires on the shore.

191
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:35,490
Halfway across, Barta passed out.

192
00:07:35,510 --> 00:07:38,569
When he came to, he was floating out of

193
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:41,305
the mouth of the bay, about fifty yards

194
00:07:41,325 --> 00:07:42,331
from a beach.

195
00:07:42,351 --> 00:07:44,042
He reached it at sunrise.

196
00:07:44,062 --> 00:07:46,436
Martyn, who Barta said was a strong

197
00:07:46,456 --> 00:07:48,146
swimmer, was never seen again.

198
00:07:48,166 --> 00:07:49,515
Rufus Smith crossed too.

199
00:07:49,535 --> 00:07:52,935
He later said that a shark bit him on the

200
00:07:52,955 --> 00:07:54,988
arm and shoulder in the water,

201
00:07:55,008 --> 00:07:57,060
and that he made it anyway.

202
00:07:58,425 --> 00:08:01,105
Glenn McDole hid near the beach with a

203
00:08:01,125 --> 00:08:02,305
wounded man named Hamrick,

204
00:08:02,325 --> 00:08:04,705
the same man Barta had tied a tourniquet

205
00:08:04,725 --> 00:08:05,905
on during the attack.

206
00:08:05,925 --> 00:08:09,420
McDole stayed with him for four days,

207
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:11,841
until Hamrick died of gangrene on the

208
00:08:11,861 --> 00:08:12,879
eighteenth of December.

209
00:08:12,899 --> 00:08:14,972
Then McDole swam the bay alone.

210
00:08:14,992 --> 00:08:17,689
Too exhausted to go on, he spent a whole

211
00:08:17,709 --> 00:08:20,425
night clinging to a fish trap in the water.

212
00:08:22,125 --> 00:08:24,689
The men who found McDole the next morning

213
00:08:24,709 --> 00:08:25,872
were prisoners themselves.

214
00:08:25,892 --> 00:08:28,724
Across the bay lay the Iwahig Penal

215
00:08:28,744 --> 00:08:30,418
Colony, a Philippine prison farm,

216
00:08:30,438 --> 00:08:32,790
and its convicts were out gathering the

217
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:34,145
fish from their traps.

218
00:08:34,165 --> 00:08:37,961
Bogue, after five days and nights in the

219
00:08:37,981 --> 00:08:39,644
jungle with nothing but rainwater,

220
00:08:39,664 --> 00:08:41,663
was rescued by the same convicts.

221
00:08:41,683 --> 00:08:44,355
The prisoners of one camp had saved the

222
00:08:44,375 --> 00:08:45,385
prisoners of another.

223
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:48,911
From there, Filipino guerrillas took over.

224
00:08:48,931 --> 00:08:51,142
They dressed the Americans in civilian

225
00:08:51,162 --> 00:08:53,746
clothes and moved them south along the

226
00:08:53,766 --> 00:08:56,721
island to the guerrilla base at Brooke's Point.

227
00:08:56,741 --> 00:09:00,120
On the sixth of January 1945,

228
00:09:00,140 --> 00:09:02,582
a Catalina flying boat landed off Brooke's

229
00:09:02,602 --> 00:09:05,395
Point and took out the first six survivors.

230
00:09:05,415 --> 00:09:07,857
On the twenty-first, a second flying boat

231
00:09:07,877 --> 00:09:10,080
came for Bogue, Barta and McDole.

232
00:09:11,325 --> 00:09:13,940
Eleven men had made it out alive:

233
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:15,446
five soldiers, three Marines,

234
00:09:15,466 --> 00:09:17,329
two airmen and one sailor.

235
00:09:17,349 --> 00:09:20,717
Nine of them were off the island by the

236
00:09:20,737 --> 00:09:21,846
end of January.

237
00:09:21,866 --> 00:09:24,125
Their sworn statements were classified secret.

238
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:27,875
By the seventh of January,

239
00:09:27,895 --> 00:09:30,269
Army intelligence had heard Nielsen's story.

240
00:09:30,289 --> 00:09:32,264
Japanese guards, facing an American

241
00:09:32,284 --> 00:09:35,456
landing, had killed an entire camp of prisoners.

242
00:09:35,476 --> 00:09:38,648
And on Luzon, the American army was about

243
00:09:38,668 --> 00:09:41,441
to land and push past another camp,

244
00:09:41,461 --> 00:09:44,398
at Cabanatuan, where about five hundred

245
00:09:44,418 --> 00:09:45,840
prisoners were still being held.

246
00:09:47,150 --> 00:09:48,860
On the twenty-sixth of January,

247
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:50,970
the guerrilla leader Major Robert Lapham

248
00:09:50,990 --> 00:09:53,330
warned that the prisoners at Cabanatuan

249
00:09:53,350 --> 00:09:55,616
were in danger of being killed.

250
00:09:55,636 --> 00:09:58,282
Four days later, Army Rangers and Filipino

251
00:09:58,302 --> 00:10:00,187
guerrillas slipped behind the Japanese

252
00:10:00,207 --> 00:10:02,853
lines, attacked the camp at night and

253
00:10:02,873 --> 00:10:05,520
carried out five hundred and thirteen prisoners.

254
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:08,567
Historians link the raid to what the Army

255
00:10:08,587 --> 00:10:11,270
had learned from Palawan.

256
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:14,243
The records do not spell out how much it weighed.

257
00:10:14,263 --> 00:10:16,919
But by then, the Army knew what guards on

258
00:10:16,939 --> 00:10:19,001
that island had done when they believed

259
00:10:19,021 --> 00:10:20,210
the Americans were coming.

260
00:10:21,925 --> 00:10:24,885
On the twenty-eighth of February 1945,

261
00:10:24,905 --> 00:10:26,907
American troops of the Forty-first

262
00:10:26,927 --> 00:10:30,545
Infantry Division landed at Puerto Princesa.

263
00:10:30,565 --> 00:10:33,241
In the empty prison camp they found

264
00:10:33,261 --> 00:10:34,782
identification tags, personal belongings

265
00:10:34,802 --> 00:10:36,322
and scraps of records.

266
00:10:36,342 --> 00:10:39,018
In March, an Army graves unit worked

267
00:10:39,038 --> 00:10:41,329
through the trenches and buried the

268
00:10:41,349 --> 00:10:44,045
remains of seventy-nine men one by one.

269
00:10:45,300 --> 00:10:47,422
In early June, through the Swiss

270
00:10:47,442 --> 00:10:49,922
government, the United States sent Tokyo a

271
00:10:49,942 --> 00:10:52,421
formal protest over the massacre of one

272
00:10:52,441 --> 00:10:54,921
hundred and fifty American prisoners of war.

273
00:10:54,941 --> 00:10:57,063
A few days later, General Douglas

274
00:10:57,083 --> 00:10:59,940
MacArthur came to see the camp for himself.

275
00:11:01,675 --> 00:11:04,651
In August 1948, sixteen Japanese soldiers

276
00:11:04,671 --> 00:11:07,675
went on trial at Yokohama for the Palawan

277
00:11:07,695 --> 00:11:08,052
massacre.

278
00:11:08,072 --> 00:11:09,182
Six were acquitted.

279
00:11:09,202 --> 00:11:11,820
The air division commander who sent the

280
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:13,704
order received life in prison.

281
00:11:13,724 --> 00:11:16,342
A sergeant of the military police was

282
00:11:16,362 --> 00:11:18,980
sentenced to death, but his sentence was

283
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:20,488
cut to thirty years.

284
00:11:20,508 --> 00:11:23,126
The camp commander, known to the prisoners

285
00:11:23,146 --> 00:11:26,140
as the Weasel, and the lieutenant who led

286
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:29,175
the soldiers to the trenches were never tried.

287
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:33,560
On the thirty-first of December 1958,

288
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:35,520
the last of the men convicted for Palawan

289
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:37,447
walked out of Sugamo prison,

290
00:11:37,467 --> 00:11:37,854
free.

291
00:11:37,874 --> 00:11:39,890
Fourteen years after the fire,

292
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:42,760
none of them was still behind bars.

293
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:46,364
If someone in your family was a prisoner

294
00:11:46,384 --> 00:11:48,649
of the Japanese, or helped the men who

295
00:11:48,669 --> 00:11:51,240
escaped from Palawan, tell us in the comments.

296
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,262
In 1952, the remains from Palawan were

297
00:11:56,282 --> 00:11:58,933
brought home and buried together in a

298
00:11:58,953 --> 00:12:00,841
single grave at Jefferson Barracks

299
00:12:00,861 --> 00:12:02,749
National Cemetery, in Saint Louis.

300
00:12:02,769 --> 00:12:05,421
One hundred and twenty-three men lie in

301
00:12:05,441 --> 00:12:07,710
that one grave, because their remains

302
00:12:07,730 --> 00:12:09,618
could not be told apart.

303
00:12:09,638 --> 00:12:13,332
In Puerto Princesa, a marker now stands on

304
00:12:13,352 --> 00:12:14,784
the site of the camp.

305
00:12:14,804 --> 00:12:17,107
It says that some one hundred and fifty

306
00:12:17,127 --> 00:12:19,139
American prisoners of war were burned to

307
00:12:19,159 --> 00:12:19,959
death there.

308
00:12:20,975 --> 00:12:23,318
And in the files of the war crimes trials,

309
00:12:23,338 --> 00:12:25,535
there is still the map that Sergeant Bogue

310
00:12:25,555 --> 00:12:27,255
drew of the camp.

311
00:12:27,275 --> 00:12:31,125
The fence, the shelters, the cliff and the bay.

312
00:12:31,145 --> 00:12:34,135
And underneath, the date he never forgot.

313
00:12:34,155 --> 00:12:36,950
Fourteen December 1944.

